A||N||12||fropuff||I think I'm outta luck.||||9||
Z||000000||fropuff||09-13-2000||10:25 PM||||Hey, I just tried to put my new Sunpro temp. gauge in my Fiero.  It looks really nice; I put it on the left side of the dash on the mirror defroster thing.  I did run into a problem though.  The wire is only 72" long (6 feet), and I don't have enough to get into the rear of the car.  I'm running it down the side of the dash and under the door.  I cannot add wire onto it, as it would screw stuff up.  Can I do anything or can't I use it?  Thanks.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Patrick||09-14-2000||02:06 AM||||<BR>Just out of curiosity, why would it "screw stuff up" to add a length of wire to the existing piece?||||reg||5
Z||000002||fropuff||09-14-2000||07:36 AM||||I'm not positive that it'll screw stuff up; maybe make the gauge inoperable.  The existing wire has a thermometer at the end, so I would have to get a whole new wire, thermometer, etc.  There's no way that the wire now will reach the rear of the car.  Where exactly does the water temp. gauge go, anyway?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||stsmithgt||09-14-2000||09:52 AM||||What was Sunpro thinking, that the engine is in the front?  I'd lengthen the wire with an equal size gauge (AWG) of wire and not worry about it.  The increased resistance from lenthening the wire will be minimal.  Just make a good connection at the point you lengthen it from.  Since I solder things everyday I'd solder the wire together with a little shrink toobing over that and a wipe of silicone sealant at each end of the shrink toobing for waterproofing. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">overkill <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> You never know when you might end up knee deep in water.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/laugh2.gif">   ||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||09-14-2000||04:06 PM||||Yeah, I don't see why you would need a wire and a thermometer. Just cut and splice.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||09-14-2000||04:35 PM||||Okay, so should I cut off the end of the wire by the thermometer, put in another piece of wire, and splice the new wire into the old and then add the thermometer?  Where exactly does the thermometer go?  I'd guess to the water pump???  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000006||HugGRRRFiero||09-14-2000||04:53 PM||||I'd splice the wire closer to the gauge because it will be inside your car and you won't have to worry about moisture corroding the solder connections.<P>For location, I would guess it should thread right in where the stock temperature gauge sensor went? Someone correct me if I'm wrong?<P>If your Fiero is a 2.5L, then that would be the sensor sticking up on the left hand side of the head close to the valve cover.  Not sure for V6, maybe someone would know if that's your case.<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">         <BR>         <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 09-14-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||09-14-2000||05:19 PM||||Yep, I've got a 6 cylinder.  I just moved the gauge to the right side in that space (cupholders for those who use it for that) It's down by my knee when I'm in it.  I need about 6 inches to let it reach the other sensors.  I thought that's where it went.  Does anyone know which one it is?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rprevo||09-14-2000||07:23 PM|||| If you are useing an electric gauge kit<BR>you can lengthen the wire but if this is the<BR>mechanicle set the water temp sensor has<BR>fluid in it, Smells like starting fluid.<BR>An automotive store called pepboys<BR>has a set of electric gauges for $39.99<BR>you can use all the wire length you want.<BR>I mounted mine in place of the center vents.<BR>Ran the wire up through the council and into <BR>the engine bay.<BR>Hope this works for you. Believe it or<BR>not that tiny copper wire is really copper<BR>tubeing.<P> Ray<P><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000009||rockcrawl||09-14-2000||08:02 PM||||rprevo is right, you need to toss that guage you have and get an electric one. The wire is actually a capilary tube containing fluid. There is no way to extend it.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fropuff||09-14-2000||08:31 PM||||I've been thinking a little.  I think I'm going to use the gauge in my winter beater, a 1980 Oldsmobile Cutlass.  I just gotta figure out how to hook it up.  I don't have one now, and I need some help hooking it up.  I also need some more help.  I just bought a new oil pressure gauge for the same vehicle.  I can't find the oil pressure port on the engine.  It's a 231 V-6, 3.8 Liter.  Can someone help me on either???||||reg||1
Z||000011||HugGRRRFiero||09-14-2000||08:43 PM||||Ohh ya.  Sorry, I was assuming you were using an electrical gauge.  Yea, don't cut into that copper tube, you'd be hosed.||||reg||11
Z||000012||Patrick||09-15-2000||05:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The <I>wire</I> is only 72" long...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Ahh...that's where you led us astray. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> It wasn't a <I>wire</I> after all. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ||||reg||1
